  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186124472,
      "id": "3bd13aa5-83a8-5b23-96cb-ea665039ea30",
      "title": "Identifying an Invertible Function From Its Graph"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "36d6dfa7-dc1a-52d1-bb2e-5062598313cf",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124472/assets/733a7c64a686be544e0731138d542005abbe149bb4fbd3b51dcbe9dfd4dc82f5.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nEach graph shows its entire domain; an open circle excludes its endpoint.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nGraph positions: **I.** upper left; **II.** upper right; **III.** lower left; **IV.** lower right.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124472/assets/71c4e8e611885d24d950602b6bbfc8338d37197dc387e698d6c7f33dfaac7b4f.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- For graph I, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, this graph shows a one-to-one function. As a result, this function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs II, III, IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. These are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "dc3dc164-9b84-5cd1-9dbe-d24c8c229ca5",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
